%N A049230 Number of n-step self-avoiding walks on the simple cubic lattice having exactly 2 pairs of nonconsecutive vertices that are adjacent in the lattice.
%C A049230 In the notation of Nemirovsky et al. (1992), a(n) = C_{n,2}(3), where C_{n,m}(d) is the number of rooted ordered n-step self-avoiding walks on the d-dimensional hypercubic lattice having exactly m nearest-neighbor contacts between nonconsecutive vertices. The terms a(1)-a(11) appear in Table I (p. 1088). - _Petros Hadjicostas_, Jan 03 2019 [Clarified by _Carlo Corti_, Aug 30 2026]
%C A049230 From _Carlo Corti_, Aug 30 2026: (Start)
%C A049230 Previous name was: Configurations of linear chains in a cubic lattice.
%C A049230 The walks are rooted at a fixed lattice vertex; rotations, reflections, and reversal are counted as distinct.
%C A049230 The computation reported in the Corti paper gives a(19)-a(22). J. H. Lee, S.-Y. Kim, and J. Lee published the coefficient corresponding to a(23), with all first-step directions included. Y.-H. Hsieh, C.-N. Chen, and C.-K. Hu published fixed-first-step coefficients; conversion from N monomers to n = N-1 steps and multiplication by 6 give a(24)-a(27). (End)
%H A049230 Carlo Corti, <a href="/A049230/b049230.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..27</a>
%H A049230 Carlo Corti, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22171393">A049230: Exact simple-cubic-lattice two-contact enumeration</a>, Zenodo archive, 2026.
%H A049230 Carlo Corti, <a href="/A049230/a049230.png">Illustration of a five-step simple-cubic self-avoiding walk with exactly two contacts</a>.
%H A049230 Y.-H. Hsieh, C.-N. Chen, and C.-K. Hu, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1051/epjconf/201610801005">Exact partition functions of interacting self-avoiding walks on lattices</a>, EPJ Web Conf. 108 (2016), 01005.
%H A049230 Y.-H. Hsieh, C.-N. Chen, and C.-K. Hu, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cpc.2016.08.006">Efficient algorithm for computing exact partition functions of lattice polymer models</a>, Comput. Phys. Commun. 209 (2016), 27-33.
%H A049230 S.-S. Huang, Y.-H. Hsieh, and C.-N. Chen, <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/polym14214536">Exact enumeration approach to estimate the theta temperature of interacting self-avoiding walks on the simple cubic lattice</a>, Polymers 14 (2022), 4536.
%H A049230 J. H. Lee, S.-Y. Kim, and J. Lee, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevE.86.011802">Exact partition function zeros of a polymer on a simple cubic lattice</a>, Phys. Rev. E 86 (2012), 011802.
%H A049230 A. M. Nemirovsky, K. F. Freed, T. Ishinabe, and J. F. Douglas, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01049010">Marriage of exact enumeration and 1/d expansion methods: lattice model of dilute polymers</a>, J. Statist. Phys., 67 (1992), 1083-1108.
%F A049230 a(n) = 24*p_{n,2}^{(2)} + 48*p_{n,2}^{(3)} = 3*A033323(n) + 48*p_{n,2}^{(3)} for n >= 1, where p_{n,2}^{(l)} is defined by Eq. (5) of Nemirovsky et al. (1992). - _Carlo Corti_, Aug 30 2026
%e A049230 For n=5, A033323(5)=32 and p_{5,2}^{(3)}=4, so a(5)=3*32+48*4=288. One representative of a genuinely three-dimensional orbit has vertices v_0=(0,0,0), v_1=(1,0,0), v_2=(1,1,0), v_3=(0,1,0), v_4=(0,1,1), v_5=(1,1,1); its two nonconsecutive contacts are {v_0,v_3} and {v_2,v_5}. See the illustration link. - _Carlo Corti_, Aug 30 2026
%Y A049230 Cf. A001412 (all n-step self-avoiding walks on the cubic lattice), A174319 (d=3, m=0), A047057 (d=3, m=1), A033323 (d=2, m=2), A046788 (d=4, m=2), A038728 (d=5, m=2).
